comments.body:
I’ve replaced the handwritten comparison loop with inline_strncmp. Regarding the two checks you mentioned, I ran some additional benchmarks. In my local first-character mismatch test, the version retaining was faster.

``` cc
if (entry[0] != first) 
  continue; 
```

This check skips entries whose first character does not match, avoiding the general comparison path in inline_strncmp and reducing per-entry overhead. Based on these results, I’d prefer to keep the first-character check.

Consider simplifying this with inline_strcmp (again, avoiding pointer arithmetic without overhead).  This sort of pattern exists throughout the codebase:

```c++
auto comp = [](char l, char r) -> int {
  return static_cast<unsigned char>(l) - static_cast<unsigned char>(r);
};
return inline_strncmp(left, right, n, comp);
```

